{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's verification criterion is a lexical-overlap threshold (<0.3), but the problem explicitly requires identifying what evidence within a run distinguishes genuine independent verification from mere restatement. Low lexical overlap is the signature of paraphrase or stylistic rewording, not of logical independence; the target even lists 'lexical diversity through paraphrasing rather than logical divergence' as a countercondition, yet offers no mechanism to separate the two beyond the same overlap metric. Thus the proposed count (lexical overlap) fails to distinguish genuine verification from restatement and does not satisfy the problem's core demand.","counterexample":[{"author_claim":"The sum of 1..n is n(n+1)/2.","checker_restatement":"An arithmetic series from one to n equals n times n plus one over two.","lexical_overlap":0.22,"logical_divergence":"none","result":"passes SRC_001's threshold but is pure restatement"}]},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 argues separation is necessary because a same-engine checker has 'perfectly correlated error terms with itself' and will pattern-match its own prior activations. But the problem context states the conjecture role group is bound to the SECOND model while the coder role group is different, and asks for evidence within a single run's record that distinguishes genuine independent verification from restatement. SRC_002 provides no countable or executable test: it asserts a psychological 'familiarity' mechanism but gives no concrete signal (e.g., a disagreement/execution outcome) to look for in the run record. It therefore fails the 'say exactly what to count or execute' requirement.","counterexample":[{"run_record":{"author_engine":"model-B","checker_engine":"model-B","outcome":"checker agrees","evidence_offered":"weights unchanged; persona shifted","distinguishable_from_restatement":false}]},{"target_alias":"SRC_003","attack":false,"case":"SRC_03 directly answers the problem: it specifies a countable/executable criterion (count cases where checker's Correction C differs from author's Claim A and execution of C succeeds where A would fail), and its counterconditions explicitly handle the 'author was already correct' and 'noise corrections' cases. The criterion is a genuine run-internal signal of independence rather than restatement, so no fault under the stated problem is established.","counterexample":null}]}